{
  "version": 3,
  "title": "What if you stepped onto Mars?",
  "series": "Mars / Make It to Sunrise",
  "narration": "One footprint. That's all you get on Mars in a T-shirt. You reach for a second breath, black out, and without rescue, you die. Rewind. Let's get this guy to sunrise. First, seal the suit. Mars barely has any air pressing on you, so the suit holds pressure around your body. Plug in an oxygen tank and take a breath. Better? Now breathe out. See the problem? That carbon dioxide is stuck in your helmet. You could have a full tank and still be in trouble. Snap a scrubber into the loop. It catches the carbon dioxide before the air comes back around. Now climb that ridge. Cold planet, sweaty astronaut. Where's all that body heat supposed to go? Water tubes under the suit carry it to the pack, which dumps the heat outside. Look at the sun. You've got enough kit for a walk, not a night. Get inside. Shut the outer hatch. Bring the pressure up. Now you can crack the helmet. Water on the shelf, food beside it, fan on the wall. Unplug that fan and the air stops circulating. So what powers it when the sun goes down? Panels charge a battery by day. At night, the battery takes over. For a longer stay, pile Martian soil over the shelter to cut the radiation dose. Morning. Same dust, same guy. This time he gets to leave that second footprint.",
  "voiceover": "episodes/mars-voice.wav",
